## 2026-07-27 - US-009

Convert the seven smaller controllers to ESM.

**Interpretation:** a mechanical module-format conversion with no
behaviour change, and there is still no unit test runner (US-019), so
per execution rule 3 no failing test was written first. Every
behavioural claim below was verified empirically against the running
app.

**What was implemented**

- All 7 controllers converted from `require` to `import` and from
  `module.exports = class X` to `export default class X`:
  audio-tracks, folder-watcher, update, prefs, media, subtitles,
  torrent.
- `subtitles-controller.js` had four *inner* lazy `require()` calls
  inside function bodies (`simple-concat`, `languagedetect`,
  `srt-to-vtt`, `iso-639-1`). Those are now static top-level imports;
  the "lazy load to keep startup fast" comment went with them.
- `src/renderer/main.js`: the seven `require('./controllers/...')` calls
  inside the `createGetter` thunks became static imports at the top of
  the file. This was forced, not cosmetic -- Babel compiles an ESM
  controller to `exports.default = class`, so `require()` would have
  returned the namespace object and `new MediaController(state)` would
  have thrown. The acceptance criteria forbid a `.default` access, so
  static imports are the only option. `createGetter` still defers
  *construction*; only module loading moved to startup.
  `playback-controller` stays on `require()` -- it is still CommonJS
  until US-010.
- Updated the stale laziness bullet in
  `src/renderer/controllers/AGENTS.md`.

**Files changed**

- 7 files in `src/renderer/controllers/`
- `src/renderer/main.js`
- `src/renderer/controllers/AGENTS.md`
- `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`

**Verification**

- `grep -rn "module\.exports\|require("` over the 7 files returns
  nothing (exit 1). `torrent-list-controller.js` and
  `playback-controller.js` are untouched, as scoped.
- `npm test` (standard + depcheck) passes, exit 0. depcheck stays green
  because the four hoisted subtitle deps are still imported.
- `npm run build` (Babel) compiles 64 files.
- `npx vite build` succeeds, and the externalized-builtin warning count
  is **149 before and 149 after** the change -- hoisting the four
  subtitle deps to top level introduced no new Node-builtin edge.
  Bundle contains one occurrence each of `Select a subtitles file.`,
  `start folder watcher`, `new version skipped by user` and
  `Download Complete`, proving subtitles/folder-watcher/update/torrent
  all reached the bundle.
- App launched via `npx electron --no-sandbox .` (`init: 726.045ms`)
  with a temporary probe appended to `build/main/index.js` (never to
  source) that dispatched one action per controller and read the state
  back. All ten returned real values, none threw:
  `toggleAudioTracksMenu` -> `showMenu true`; `toggleSubtitlesMenu` ->
  `true`; `checkForSubtitles` -> no throw; `mediaMouseMoved` ->
  timestamp; `mediaStalled` -> `isStalled true`; `updateAvailable
  '99.0.0'` -> `modal.id === 'update-available-modal'`; `skipVersion` ->
  `skippedVersions ['99.0.0']`; folder watcher start+stop -> sent;
  `preferences` -> `location.url() === 'preferences'`;
  `updatePreferences` -> pref round-tripped.
- `torrent-controller` got a real end-to-end check rather than a
  synthetic dispatch: it is constructed inside `setupIpc()` before
  `console.timeEnd('init')`, so the `init:` line alone proves the
  default export resolves. On top of that the probe dispatched
  `addTorrent` with the Sintel magnet link; `torrentParsed` and
  `torrentMetadata` both fired (the new entry came back with its
  infoHash and 11 files). The probe then deleted it and the saved
  torrent count returned to its baseline of 11.
- The only severity-3 renderer console lines are the two pre-existing
  ones already recorded under US-005/US-008 (the `electron.remote`
  `getCurrentWindow` failure and the Material-UI TextField id
  warning). Neither is new.
- `npm run build` was re-run afterwards; `build/main/index.js` has 0
  `PROBE` matches and no dev electron process is left running.
- The six-step manual smoke test was **not** run -- this session is
  non-interactive. Residual risk sits on the three areas the criteria
  call out: preferences changes were exercised only via a no-op
  round-trip of one pref (the real `setDefaultFileHandler` /`setStartup`
  IPC branches were not hit), subtitles was exercised via the menu
  toggle and an early-returning `checkForSubtitles` but no actual .srt
  file was ever parsed through the hoisted `srt-to-vtt` /
  `simple-concat` path, and the torrent add stopped at metadata --
  nothing was downloaded or played.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- **Converting a controller to ESM forces `main.js` to change too.**
  The `createGetter(() => { const X = require(...); return new X() })`
  shape silently breaks the moment the required module becomes ESM:
  Babel's `exports.default` makes `require()` return the namespace
  object, so `new X()` throws `X is not a constructor` at first
  dispatch, not at startup. US-010 has the same coupling for
  `playback-controller` -- convert the file and its `main.js` thunk in
  the same commit.
- **`import` in `main.js` deletes the startup-laziness optimisation,
  not just the syntax.** Worth stating plainly because the old comment
  claimed the laziness was deliberate perf work. Measured cost: `init:`
  went 664ms (US-008) -> 726ms (now), inside run-to-run noise, so the
  laziness was not buying much. Don't reintroduce inner `require`s to
  chase it.
- **Externalized-builtin warning *count* is the cheap regression check
  for hoisting deps.** `npx vite build 2>&1 | grep -c externalized`
  before and after a change is a one-line way to prove a newly-static
  import did not drag a Node builtin into the graph. Much faster than
  re-reading `docs/vite-baseline.md`.
- **`window.dispatch` plus a state read is enough evidence for any
  controller story.** Every controller action is reachable as
  `window.dispatch('<actionName>', ...)` (the names are the keys of
  `dispatchHandlers` in `main.js`), and most mutate something readable
  under `window.state`. Wrap each in try/catch inside one
  `executeJavaScript` call and return JSON -- a thrown
  `X is not a constructor` is exactly what a broken default export
  looks like.
- **The dev app shares the real saved-state file.** `addTorrent` in a
  probe writes to the user's actual torrent list, so read the baseline
  count first, filter by the infoHash you added, `deleteTorrent` it
  with `deleteData: false`, and assert the count came back. Also: probe
  output includes the user's torrent names -- don't paste raw probe
  logs into `progress.log`.
