## 2026-07-28 - US-025

Implement the data-driven `menu.js` API.

**What was implemented**

- `src/renderer/lib/platform/menu.js` is now real. `showContextMenu`
  maps an array of `{ type, label, click, enabled }` descriptors to
  `MenuItem` options, builds a `remote.Menu`, and pops it up parented
  to `getCurrentWindow()`. Named import from `@electron/remote`
  (`Menu`, `MenuItem`, `getCurrentWindow`), per the directory's
  convention, which is also what makes it unit-testable.
- Validation happens for the whole array *before* the `Menu` is
  constructed: an unknown `type` or a missing `label` on a normal item
  rejects, and nothing half-built ever reaches the screen.
- Each caller `click` is wrapped in a zero-arg closure, so Electron's
  `(menuItem, browserWindow, event)` arguments stop at the shim. FR-12
  is about objects not crossing the boundary in either direction.
- `enabled` defaults to `true`; only an explicit `false` disables.
- `test/unit/platform-menu.js`: 15 assertions with `@electron/remote`
  stubbed -- item count and label order for the real torrent context
  menu, popup parenting, separator shape, `enabled` defaulting, the
  click-arity guarantee, both rejection paths, and the export surface.
- `docs/platform-shim.md`: the descriptor table, the click-arity and
  validation-order rules, and the explicit no-submenu decision.
- `src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md` and `test/AGENTS.md` updated.

**Interpretation of the acceptance criteria**

- "Separators and submenus are representable if the existing menus use
  them." Verified by grepping `src/renderer/` for `MenuItem` /
  `new remote.Menu`: `torrent-list-controller.js:258-310` is the only
  menu in the renderer, and it has separators but **no submenus**. So
  separators are implemented and submenus are deliberately not, on the
  same "no more than the renderer does today" rule `fs.js` follows.
  Documented in `docs/platform-shim.md` rather than left implicit.
- "the nine-item torrent context menu ... produces nine menu items."
  The real menu is not nine items. Counting the `menu.append` calls at
  `:258-310` gives **ten** when `torrentSummary.files` is set (seven
  items plus three separators) and **eight** when it is not. "Nine" in
  the story text is a miscount carried over from US-018. The test
  asserts the stronger, true property -- one `MenuItem` per descriptor
  for the full ten-descriptor menu, with labels in order -- rather
  than a number that does not match the code. Flagging it here rather
  than editing the criterion.
- No call site was migrated (US-029 does that), so
  `torrent-list-controller.js` is untouched.

**Files changed**

- `src/renderer/lib/platform/menu.js`
- `test/unit/platform-menu.js` (new)
- `docs/platform-shim.md`
- `src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md`, `test/AGENTS.md`
- `specs/prd.json` (US-025 `passes: true`)

**Verification**

`npm test` green: standard, depcheck, 127 unit assertions. `npm run
build` (Babel + Vite) clean -- no new externalization warnings. The
implementation was confirmed to be doing the work: stashing only
`menu.js` and rebuilding drops the new file to 2 passing / 7 failing.
No app launch this session; nothing renderer-visible changed, since
the module still has no importers.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **A `require.cache` stub's `exports` object is bound once**, at
    the first require of the module under test. Babel keeps the result
    of its single `require()` call, so a `reset()` that rebuilds the
    entire stub leaves the module holding the *first* one -- the
    symptom is a recorder that stays empty while the code under test
    is demonstrably running. `platform-window.js` gets away with it
    because it swaps state *behind* a stable `getCurrentWindow`
    function. Do the same: stable functions/classes (or getters) on
    one `exports` object, mutable state behind them. Even an
    `exports` *getter* on the cache entry is too late.
  - A class-valued Electron export (`Menu`, `MenuItem`) stubs exactly
    like a function one -- Babel emits `new _remote.Menu()`, a
    per-call-site property read, same as everything else here.
  - The remaining Phase 2 stories are all call-site migrations now;
    the shim itself is complete as of this commit.
