    extensions.api.cast_channel.CastMessage` from the bundled
    chromecast protobuf stack. It does not stop the app (init
    completes, the UI renders, probes work). It is dormant code being
    reached, not a regression from this story, and it is unowned by
    any current story -- likely a Vite-bundling casualty in
    `lib/cast.js`, whose deps were flagged as a deferred casualty in
    the shim AGENTS.md.
  - **Probing a *native* modal non-interactively.**
    `showOpenDialogSync` blocks the whole main process. So
    `wc.executeJavaScript("window.dispatch('openSubtitles')")` followed
    by any further `executeJavaScript` or `setTimeout` in the appended
    probe block produces *silence* -- and that silence is the
    evidence the dialog opened. Even `kill <pid>` does not land while
    the dialog is up; `pkill -f "$PWD/node_modules/"` is needed. Plan
    the blocking probe LAST in a launch, because nothing after it
    runs.
  - **`dispatch()` re-renders synchronously, shim calls do not.**
    `dispatch(action)` runs the handler then `update()` in the same
    turn, so state written inside a `.then()` misses that render.
    End the continuation with `dispatch('update')` (there is a no-op
    `update` handler for exactly this) unless it already dispatches
    something that re-renders. `path-selector.js` needs no such call:
    its `onChange` is `dispatch('updatePreferences', ...)`.
  - **Boundary tests should name concerns, not specifiers.** A file
    can hold two shim concerns owned by two different stories
    (`torrent-list-controller.js` holds dialog and menu). Banning the
    import specifier makes the story unpassable; banning
    `remote.dialog` and `getCurrentWindow().isMaximized` does not.
---

## 2026-07-28 - US-028

Migrate every renderer `fs` call site onto `lib/platform/fs.js`.

**What was implemented**

- `lib/migrations.js`: dropped `import fs, { copyFileSync } from 'fs'`
  for `import * as fs from './platform/fs'`. `run()` is now `async`;
  `migrate_0_7_0`, `migrate_0_12_0` and `migrate_0_17_2` -- the three
  that touch the filesystem -- are `async` and are `await`ed one at a
  time in the existing version order. The two `forEach` loops holding
  fs work became `for..of` so the `await` is inside the loop rather
  than inside a callback the loop never waits for.
  `statSync`-in-a-try became `await fs.exists()`.
- `lib/state.js`: `setupStateSaved()` and its inner
  `createTorrentObject()` are `async`; both `mkdir`s are awaited before
  the copies start. `load(cb)` -- already an `async function` -- now
  awaits `setupStateSaved()` and `migrations.run()` before firing `cb`,
  so nothing starts torrenting off a half-migrated config. The inner
  `onSavedState` helper was inlined; it existed only to be called from
  two places, one of which was the error path.
- `main.js`: `checkDownloadPath()` uses `platformFs.stat().then/catch`,
  with `stat.isDirectory` as a boolean.
- `controllers/torrent-list-controller.js`: four sites -- the
  path-still-there `stat`, `findFilesRecursive`'s `stat`+`readdir`,
  `deleteFile`'s `unlink`, and `saveTorrentFileAs`'s
  `readFile`+`writeFile`, which folds into the dialog promise chain
  that already ends in `.catch(err => dispatch('error', err))`.
- `controllers/subtitles-controller.js`: `createReadStream(...).pipe(
  srtToVtt())` became a bare `srtToVtt()` transform fed by
  `fs.readFile()`. `concat()` is registered before the write, so no
  chunk is missed.
- `docs/platform-shim.md`, `test/AGENTS.md` updated.

**Tests (written first, both failing before the change)**

- `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`: new check banning any `fs`,
  `fs/promises` or `original-fs` import under `src/renderer/` outside
  `lib/platform/`, with `webtorrent.js` exempt per FR-4. Failed on
  exactly the five files the story names.
- `test/unit/migrations.js` (new): runs the real migrations against a
  real temp profile, with `build/config` stubbed via `require.cache`.
  Asserts `run()` returns a promise, that the 0.7.0 and 0.17.2 file
  copies are on disk by the time it resolves, that the version-ordered
  chain still works (0.7.2 creating `prefs` before 0.11.0 reads it),
  and that `setDefaultFileHandler` is still sent. Failed with
  `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'then')`.

**Files changed**

- `src/renderer/main.js`, `src/renderer/lib/state.js`,
  `src/renderer/lib/migrations.js`,
  `src/renderer/controllers/torrent-list-controller.js`,
  `src/renderer/controllers/subtitles-controller.js`
- `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`, `test/unit/migrations.js` (new)
- `docs/platform-shim.md`, `test/AGENTS.md`, `progress.log`,
  `specs/prd.json`

**Verification**

- `npm test` passes: 337 assertions, exit 0.
- `npm run lint` clean.
- `npm run build` (Babel + Vite) clean, 0 `has been externalized`
  warnings.
- Empirical, two launches under `HOME=/tmp/wt-fresh-home`:
  1. Fresh profile. Log shows `Missing config file: Creating new one`,
     then `init: 858ms`. The profile ends up with `config.json`
     (`version 0.24.0`, 5 torrents, `prefs` present), 5 files in
     `Posters/` and 5 in `Torrents/` -- so the awaited `mkdir`s and
     copies in `setupStateSaved()` all completed.
  2. Same profile, `config.json` hand-staled to `version: "0.20.0"`
     with `soundNotifications` and `externalPlayerPath` deleted and
     `downloadPath` set to a marker. Relaunch shows no
     `Missing config file` line (state persisted and was read), the
     5 torrents and the marker `downloadPath` survive, and
     `soundNotifications: true` / `externalPlayerPath: ""` /
     `version 0.24.0` are back -- migrations ran and their result was
     saved.
  With the `console-message` hook installed on `build/main/index.js`,
  the only severity-3 renderer line is the known dormant
  `extensions.api.cast_channel.CastMessage` from `Cast.init()`.
  `build/` was regenerated with `npm run build` afterwards.
- **The six-step manual smoke test was NOT run** -- it needs a real
  interactive session. The two launches above cover the two things the
  story's smoke criterion actually names (persistence across relaunch,
  migrations on a fresh profile). The residual risk is in the paths
  neither launch exercises: saving a .torrent file via the Save-As
  dialog, drag-and-drop folder seeding (`findFilesRecursive`), and
  loading a subtitle file.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- **A fresh profile is one env var away.** `application-config` reads
  `process.env.HOME` at call time, so
  `HOME=/tmp/whatever npx electron --no-sandbox .` gives a completely
  clean config directory without touching the real one. Hand-editing
  the `version` field in that profile's `config.json` between launches
  is how you make migrations run on demand. This is the only practical
  way to test anything in `lib/state.js` or `lib/migrations.js`
  end-to-end.
- **`parse-torrent` 9 rejects a bare `Uint8Array`** with
  `Invalid torrent identifier`; it wants a `Buffer`. Wrap without
  copying: `Buffer.from(u.buffer, u.byteOffset, u.byteLength)`. The
  shim deliberately hands out `Uint8Array` (no Node objects across the
  boundary), so any consumer that sniffs for `Buffer` needs this at the
  call site. Check the consumer before assuming a `Uint8Array` is
  interchangeable.
- **`await` inside `.forEach()` does nothing.** Both migrations that
  copy files did their work in a `forEach` callback; converting to
  `for..of` is not cosmetic, it is the difference between `run()`
  resolving after the copies and resolving before them. Grep for
  `forEach` whenever you make a function async.
- **Stub `build/config` the same way you stub `electron`.** Seed
  `require.cache[require.resolve('../../build/config')]` with
  `{ __esModule: true, default: {...} }` -- the `__esModule` flag
  matters because every renderer module imports config as a default
  import. That is what makes `lib/state.js` and `lib/migrations.js`
  unit-testable at all: point `POSTER_PATH`/`TORRENT_PATH` at a
  `mkdtemp` directory and let the real fs calls run.
- **The migration chain is order-coupled in both directions.**
  `migrate_0_7_0` *deletes* `ts.files`, which `migrate_0_17_2` then
  reads -- so a single test fixture cannot exercise both. Two fixtures
  at two starting versions is the honest shape; one fixture at
  `version: 1` crashes exactly the way a real pre-0.7.0 config with a
  WIRED CD entry would.
- **A stream can be replaced without replacing its consumer.**
  `srt-to-vtt` is a transform, so dropping `createReadStream` only
  means constructing the transform directly and `.end()`ing it with the
  bytes. Register the `concat()` consumer before writing.
