  controller; that specifier no longer appears in it at all. Labels,
  order, the `enabled` condition on "Save Torrent File As...", the
  three separators and the manual `checkmark ` prefix on "Sort by
  Name" are byte-identical to before.
- New test `test/unit/torrent-context-menu.js` (45 assertions) --
  written first and failing on
  `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'Menu')`. It compiles
  the controller with `@babel/core` into
  `build/renderer/controllers/`, stubs `lib/platform/menu` and
  `lib/platform/clipboard` through `require.cache`, and drives
  `openTorrentContextMenu()` against a fixture state. It asserts the
  descriptor array is plain objects, both label orders (with and
  without `files`), separators carrying nothing else, the `enabled`
  rule in both directions, the check-mark label, and what each of the
  seven `click` handlers does (dispatch args, the `showItemInFolder`
  IPC send, both clipboard strings) -- plus that every `click` takes
  zero parameters.
- `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` gains the menu concern:
  `new remote.Menu` / `new remote.MenuItem` (both the `remote.` and
  `electron.remote.` spellings) and a named `Menu`/`MenuItem` import
  from `@electron/remote`.
- Docs: `docs/platform-shim.md` records the migration under
  `menu.js`; `test/AGENTS.md` gains the controller-compilation recipe
  and drops the now-stale note that a blanket `@electron/remote` ban
  would fail because of `remote.Menu`.

**Verification**

- `npm test` green: 422 assertions, standard and depcheck clean.
- `npm run build` clean, 0 `has been externalized` warnings, and the
  minified renderer bundle contains
  `platform/menu: unsupported item type` -- the shim reached the Vite
  graph, which it had not before (nothing imported it).
- Launch check: `init: 531ms`, `'Main Window'`, no new errors in the
  log; process tree killed and `pgrep` verified clean.
- **The smoke-test criterion ("every item in the torrent row context
  menu exercised") was NOT run.** A native context menu needs a real
  right-click and then blocks the main process, so it cannot be driven
  non-interactively (`openTorrentContextMenu` is on the do-not-dispatch
  list in `controllers/AGENTS.md` for that reason). The unit test
  covers what each item *does* -- the residual risk is purely that the
  menu renders/pops up, which is `showContextMenu`'s two lines,
  unchanged since US-025.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- **Compiling one ignored file in the test is cheaper than moving it
  into the build.** Adding `controllers/` to `build:main` would have
  worked too, but it changes what ships and what `build/` contains for
  every future session. A per-test `transformFileSync` is contained to
  the test, and emitting into `build/renderer/controllers/` is the
  whole trick -- relative imports resolve from the *output* location.
- **`setDispatch()` beats stubbing the dispatcher.** `lib/dispatcher`
  already has an injection point, so the real module stays in the
  graph and only the sink is a test double. Look for an existing seam
  before seeding `require.cache`.
- **Counting menu items is a trap.** The story calls it "nine-item";
  the menu is 7 normal items plus 3 separators (10 entries) with
  `files`, 8 without. Assert the label array, not a count.
