grepped for `electron.shell`, not for the channel names.

**No `removeListener` existed** at any of these call sites (the AC
anticipates some), so nothing needed the unsubscribe function. The only
listener registrations left in these files are `wt-*` `.once` calls.

### Files changed
- `src/renderer/lib/dispatcher.js`, `src/renderer/lib/migrations.js`
- `src/renderer/controllers/{media,torrent,folder-watcher,prefs,
  torrent-list,playback}-controller.js`
- `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` (US-030 concern + a wt-* counter-check)
- `test/unit/os-ipc-call-sites.js` (new, behavioural)
- `test/unit/migrations.js`, `test/unit/torrent-context-menu.js` (retargeted)
- `docs/platform-shim.md`, `test/AGENTS.md`,
  `src/renderer/controllers/AGENTS.md`, `specs/prd.json`

### Verification
`npm test` 459/459, `npm run lint` clean, `npm run build` (both halves)
clean, and a background launch check reached `'Main Window'` and
`init: 705.85ms` with no new errors. **The six-step manual smoke test was
not run** -- it needs an interactive session. The AC calls out
preferences changes and folder-watcher pickup as the risk; both are
covered by real unit tests in `test/unit/os-ipc-call-sites.js`
(setup/destroy toggling `setAllowNav`, `update()` routing to
`setDefaultFileHandler`/`setStartup`, `start`/`stop` reaching the
watcher exports), which is the closest honest substitute. The
uncovered risk is the playback-controller and torrent-controller
notification sites, which are fire-and-forget and only observable by
watching the dock.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **`tape test/unit/*.js` is ONE process and requires every file
    before running any test.** Two things leak across files as a
    result, and both bit this story:
    `setDispatch()` (last file loaded wins for the whole run -- call it
    inside the per-test `reset()`), and `require.cache` stubs of built
    shim modules (`platform-ipc-os.js` tests the real `ipc-os`, so a
    stub left at its path fails it). Withdraw a shim stub immediately
    after requiring the module under test; Babel has already bound the
    module object by then, so the test keeps its stub. Glob order is
    alphabetical, which is why this only shows up as "my new file
    broke a file I never touched".
  - **Migrating a call site can break another story's test.** US-029's
    `torrent-context-menu.js` asserted on `record.sent` for
    `showItemInFolder`; moving that channel to `shell.js` moved the
    assertion too. Grep the tests for the channel name before
    migrating, not after.
  - **`controllers/AGENTS.md` already had the rule that matters here:**
    state written inside a `.then()` lands after the render, so end the
    continuation with `dispatch('update')`. `media-controller.mediaError`
    needed it (the unsupported-media modal). The pre-shim code had the
    same latency through `ipcRenderer.once` and never fixed it -- a
    migration is a good moment to apply the rule, but say so, because it
    is a behaviour change, not a pure refactor.
  - **A boundary test can assert what did *not* move.** The story
    explicitly defers `wt-*`, so `platform-boundary.js` now asserts both
    that no non-`wt-` channel survives *and* that the four
    engine-facing controllers still speak `wt-*`. That catches
    over-migration, which lint and a launch check never would.
