## 2026-07-28 - US-031
- Migrated every `ipcRenderer` call site in `src/renderer/main.js` onto
  the platform shim, and removed the file's `import electron from
  'electron'`.
- The OS channels went to `lib/platform/ipc-os.js`: `setTitle`,
  `setProgress`, `setBadge` in `updateElectron()`;
  `openTorrentFileDialog`, `openFilesDialog` and `toggleFullScreen` in
  the dispatch table; `setAspectRatio` + `setBounds` in
  `applyDimensions()` and `setAspectRatio` again in
  `onFullscreenChanged()`; `signalReady` / `signalStateSaved` and the
  five inbound listeners in `setupIpc()`.
- `setupIpc()` is now `async`. It `await Promise.all([...])`s all
  eighteen listener registrations, then `await ipcOs.signalReady()`.
  Ordering is the point: `ipcReady` is what tells the main process the
  renderer is listening, and while the Electron shim registers
  synchronously before resolving, a non-Electron host is free not to.
  The single caller became `reportErrors(setupIpc())`.
- `onFullscreenChanged(e, isFullScreen)` and
  `onWindowBoundsChanged(e, newBounds)` lost their leading event
  parameter -- the shim's `listen()` strips it.
- **Scope call: `main.js`'s thirteen `wt-*` listeners moved to
  `ipc-engine.js` too, ahead of US-032.** AC-2 requires `main.js` to
  stop importing `electron`, and those listeners were the only thing
  still holding the import. Doing only the OS half would have left a
  direct Electron import in the renderer entry point purely to respect
  a story boundary. US-032 still owns the four engine-facing
  controllers, which is the bulk of it. Recorded in
  `docs/platform-shim.md` and in the boundary test's comments.
- Tests: new `test/unit/main-ipc-call-sites.js` (17 assertions).
  `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` widened -- the OS-IPC check now
  walks the whole renderer minus `webtorrent.js` instead of a
  hand-maintained file list, and its `wt-*` counterpart drops
  `main.js`. Both written failing first (12 failures), then
  implemented to green. Full suite 503/503, `standard` clean.
- Files changed: `src/renderer/main.js`,
  `src/renderer/lib/platform/ipc-os.js` (header comment),
  `src/renderer/lib/platform/ipc-engine.js` (header comment),
  `docs/platform-shim.md`, `test/AGENTS.md`,
  `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`,
  `test/unit/main-ipc-call-sites.js`, `specs/prd.json`.
- **Verification.** Launch check with the renderer-console hook
  appended to `build/main/index.js`. Two pieces of direct evidence for
  the risky part:
  - `init: 749.898ms` in the log is `console.timeEnd('init')` from
    `app.once('ipcReady', ...)` in `src/main/index.js:114`, so
    `signalReady()` genuinely reached the main process after the
    awaited registrations.
  - `RENDERER-CONSOLE: [1,"dispatch: %s %o stateSaveImmediate "]` is
    the main process pushing a dispatch *into* the renderer, so
    `ipcOs.onDispatch` works end to end.
  The only severity-3 line is the known dormant Cast error. **The
  six-step manual smoke test (AC-5) was NOT run** -- it cannot be, in a
  non-interactive session. The steps carrying residual risk are the
  ones no probe above touched: dock badge/progress during a real
  download (`setBadge`/`setProgress`), the fullscreen round trip
  (`onFullscreenChanged` -> `setAspectRatio`), and the window-bounds
  save on resize.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **`init: <n>ms` in the launch log is not the renderer's.** There
    are now three `init:` timers: the two renderer ones already noted
    above, plus one in the *main* process that stops inside
    `app.once('ipcReady')`. That third one is a free, precise assertion
    that the renderer's ready signal arrived -- useful for any story
    that touches renderer->main handshake ordering.
  - **Main-to-renderer IPC can be observed without any probe.** The
    app dispatches `stateSaveImmediate` into the renderer on quit
    (`src/main/index.js:125`), and the renderer's own `dispatch()`
    logs it. So killing the launch check is itself a live test of the
    inbound `dispatch` channel -- read the tail of the log rather than
    writing an `executeJavaScript` probe for it.
  - **A shim listener registration is synchronous in Electron but
    promised by contract.** Anything that has to happen *after*
    listeners are live (here, `ipcReady`) must await them, or the
    ordering silently depends on an implementation detail the Tauri
    host is free to break. Await the registrations; don't rely on
    `listen()` returning before the microtask queue drains.
  - **`main.js` is not unit-testable at all**, not even with US-030's
    compile-the-file-yourself recipe -- its module body calls
    `State.load()` and it drags in React plus the whole controller
    graph. The substitute that still has teeth: resolve each
    `shim.member` the source references against the `export function`
    names in the shim source, so a typo fails a test instead of
    becoming `undefined` at runtime. See `test/AGENTS.md`.
  - **A per-story file list in a boundary test is a maintenance
    burden that ends.** Once the last file in a concern is migrated,
    replace the list with a walk of the whole tree; keep a named list
    only for the inverse assertion (what has *not* moved yet), where
    the list failing on change is the feature.
