Verification: `npm test` green (570 assertions), `npm run lint` clean,
`npm run build` clean with 0 externalization warnings, and a launch
check showing `'Main Window'` and `init: 811ms` with no new severity-3
console errors. The six-step manual smoke test was **not** run --
non-interactive session. AC-6 names torrent add, progress updates,
file selection and streaming start/stop; the unit tests cover the
call sites and arguments for all four, but nothing here proves the
engine end actually reacts, so that is the residual risk.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **`wt-server-${infoHash}` has no subscriber in the renderer UI.**
    `webtorrent.js` sends it, but only `wt-server-running` is listened
    for. `ipcEngine.onServerRunningFor` therefore has zero call sites
    after this story -- that is not an omission, and US-033 should
    document the channel as part of the protocol regardless.
  - **Two unit files compiling the same controller collide.** The
    compiled copy is keyed in `require.cache` by its emitted path, so
    whichever test file loads first (glob order) hands its stubs to
    the other. `os-ipc-call-sites.js` and `torrent-context-menu.js`
    already compile `media-controller.js` / `torrent-list-controller.js`
    into `build/renderer/controllers/`; the fix is to emit under a
    suffixed file name in the same directory (the *directory* is what
    resolves `../lib/...`, not the name).
  - **Stub every built module the controller graph reaches, not just
    the shim under test.** Letting a real shim module load in your
    file binds *your* `electron` stub into it for the whole run, and
    the failure surfaces in an unrelated later file. Two real cases
    here: `torrent-list-controller` -> `lib/platform/clipboard` broke
    `platform-clipboard.js`, and `playback-controller` -> `lib/state`
    -> `lib/migrations` broke `migrations.js`. Symptom is always "a
    test I never touched now fails"; the cause is always the first
    require winning.
  - **Migrating a file can break another story's test through a new
    import.** `media-controller` gaining `ipc-engine` made
    `os-ipc-call-sites.js` load the real `ipc-engine`, which then
    failed `platform-ipc-engine.js`. Whenever you add a shim import to
    a file some other unit test compiles, that test needs the new
    module stubbed too.
  - **A `\bipcRenderer\b` ban catches prose.** `lib/state.js` mentions
    the word in a comment. Match `ipcRenderer\s*\.` for the usage ban
    and let the import check cover the rest.
