## 2026-07-28 - US-033

Document the `wt-*` protocol at the shim boundary.

**Interpretation:** this is a documentation story. Per execution rule 3
and the global "do not write tests for docs" rule, no failing test was
written first -- a test asserting the doc's text against a channel list
would be exactly the brittle doc test that rule forbids, and the
existing `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` already gates the property
that matters (no `wt-*` literal outside `webtorrent.js` and
`ipc-engine.js`). The cross-check criterion was satisfied empirically
with grep instead, and the counts are recorded in the doc so a future
session can re-run the same grep and compare.

**What was implemented**

- Added a `## The wt-* protocol` section to `docs/platform-shim.md`,
  after the `ipc-engine.js` call-site tables and before the exempt-files
  section. It covers: the transport (the `ipcMain.emit` relay), the two
  directions, a commands table and an events table with payload and
  one-shot/subscription/fire-and-forget for every channel, the two
  dynamic families as families, the five payload shapes (`TorrentInfo`,
  `Progress`, `ServerInfo`, `FileModtimes`, `CreateOptions`,
  `AudioMetadata`), and the cross-check against what the renderer
  actually sends.
- Stated plainly at the top of that section that it is the contract any
  librqbit implementation must satisfy.
- Updated the `ipc-engine.js` section's forward reference (it said
  US-033 would record the protocol "once the call sites have moved").
- Corrected `torrentKey` in `ipc-engine.js`'s JSDoc from `string` to
  `number` in all 13 places. It is `state.nextTorrentKey++`, seeded at
  `1` in `lib/state.js:64`. Documenting the protocol is the point of the
  story, and the JSDoc is part of that documentation.
- Added a pointer to the new section in
  `src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md`.

**Files changed**

- `docs/platform-shim.md`
- `src/renderer/lib/platform/ipc-engine.js` (JSDoc types only)
- `src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md`
- `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`

**Verification**

- `npm run lint` clean.
- `npm test` green: 570/570, standard + depcheck + test-unit.
- No app launch: nothing behavioural changed. The only code edit is
  JSDoc comment text.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- **`wt-new-torrent` and `wt-uncaught-error` have no listener
  anywhere.** The pre-existing note in `docs/platform-shim.md` said
  their "only receiver is `src/main/`", which is only true of the
  `ipcMain.emit` relay -- it forwards them to the main window on the
  `wt-` prefix and nothing there subscribes. Grep the whole repo before
  repeating a claim about who consumes a channel; the relay makes
  "reaches `src/main/`" and "is handled" look identical.
- **`bitfield` is the one non-JSON payload field, and the UI already
  copes.** `pages/player-page.js:943` calls
  `BitField.prototype.get.call(prog.bitfield, i)`, not
  `prog.bitfield.get(i)`, because structured clone strips the
  prototype. Do not "fix" that borrowed method call -- it is what makes
  a plain-object bitfield work, and it is what will let a librqbit host
  send JSON there.
- **`torrentKey` is a number.** Several JSDoc blocks said `string`.
  Nothing breaks (it is opaque and only echoed back), but any typed
  reimplementation of the protocol would have inherited the error.
- **The renderer's `wt-*` inventory, for re-checking:** 24 distinct
  static channels plus the two dynamic family prefixes across
  `src/renderer/**/*.js`; all 24 in `webtorrent.js`, 22 of them in
  `ipc-engine.js`. Command to reproduce:
  `grep -rhoE "'wt-[a-z-]*[a-z]'" --include=*.js src/renderer/ |
  sort -u`. Note the trailing `[a-z]` -- without it the family prefixes
  `'wt-ready-'` and `'wt-server-'` are counted as static channels and
  the total reads 26.
