  by index, captures video frames through a new `lib/video-frame.js`,
  and writes the poster itself with `platform/fs` to
  `path.join(config.POSTER_PATH, infoHash + extension)` -- the same
  name the engine used. New API: `choosePosterSource(files)` (pure),
  default `torrentPoster(summary, serverURL)`, and
  `generateTorrentPoster(summary)` (the whole job, serialized, quiet on
  failure, 60s cap).
- `src/renderer/lib/video-frame.js` (new): detached `<video>` plus a
  canvas, `canvas.toBlob('image/jpeg')`. `capture-frame` dropped from
  `package.json` -- it returned a Node `Buffer` (ADR-007).
- `wt-generate-torrent-poster` and `wt-poster` deleted from
  `ipc-engine.js` (`generateTorrentPoster`, `onPosterSaved`), from
  `main.js`, from both engines, and from `docs/platform-shim.md`.
- `wt-start-server`/`wt-stop-server` are per-torrent now, in
  `src/engine/index.js` and `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` both: a `Map`
  keyed by info hash, each entry counting holders, always replying, and
  destroyed by its last release.
- `torrent-controller.js` calls the poster module; `torrentPosterSaved`
  stays as the recording step. `playback-controller.js` and
  `media-controller.js` moved onto `onServerRunningFor`, and
  `torrentServerRunning` ignores an info that is not the playing
  torrent's.
- Tests: new `test/unit/poster.js` (37 assertions, written first --
  verified red against `HEAD`'s module with
  `choosePosterSource is not a function`). Updated
  `engine-ipc-call-sites`, `os-ipc-call-sites`, `main-ipc-call-sites`,
  `platform-ipc-engine` and `wire` for the changed protocol, and
  rewrote the never-registered `test/test-select-poster.js` onto the
  selection half, which is all of it that can run under plain Node.
- `npm test` green (1683 assertions), `npm run lint` clean.

**Empirical evidence for the "video, audio and image each produce a
poster" criterion** (the six-step manual smoke test was NOT run -- this
session is non-interactive; the risk it carries is the end-to-end path
from a newly added torrent, i.e. the engine actually starting a server
on `wt-start-server` and the thumbnail appearing in the list): with a
temporary `window.__posterProbe` in `src/renderer/main.js` and a probe
HTTP server appended to `build/main/index.js`, one launch produced
`PROBE-RESULT video: 3191 bytes .jpg | image: 46115 bytes .jpg |
audio: 46115 bytes .jpg`. So a real Chromium decode of
`test/resources/monitor-test.mp4` yielded a JPEG, and `fetch` from the
`file://` renderer to a localhost HTTP server worked. The only
severity-3 renderer console line was the known dormant Cast one. The
probe was reverted and the app rebuilt; no processes left.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **A quoted `wt-` string in a *comment* fails the boundary scan.**
    `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` greps `/['"`]wt-/` over every
    renderer source outside the shim, and it cannot tell code from
    prose. Write channel names bare in comments outside
    `lib/platform/` -- backticks count as quotes.
  - **`state.server` is the playing torrent's server, and now needs
    filtering.** Anything that starts a server makes the engine
    broadcast `wt-server-running`, so a second starter (poster
    generation) would have clobbered playback's URL. The per-torrent
    `wt-server-${infoHash}` channel existed for exactly this and had no
    caller until now; `onServerRunningFor` is the right subscription
    for "I want *my* server", and the broadcast is only safe for the
    one listener that filters (`torrentServerRunning`).
  - **A "start" command that can have two holders needs a count, not a
    flag.** The old `if (server) return` meant a second request for a
    different torrent never replied at all, and a naive per-torrent map
    would let either holder's stop destroy the other's stream. Counting
    holders, replying even when the server is already up, and
    destroying on the last release is the shape.
  - **`closePlayer()` resets `state.playing` before it stops the
    server**, so any argument derived from the playing torrent has to
    be read into a local first. Same trap for anything else that grows
    an argument out of play state.
  - **Probing a renderer module non-interactively:** add a
    `window.__x = require(...)` line to `src/renderer/main.js`,
    `npm run build`, append the probe (an HTTP server plus a
    `wc.executeJavaScript` that returns a string) to
    `build/main/index.js`, launch, read the resolved value off stdout,
    then restore the source and rebuild. This is how a "does the real
    webview decode it" criterion gets actual evidence rather than an
    argument. Serve `Range` requests: Chromium's media loader asks for
    them.
