## 2026-07-29 - US-046

Extend the boundary rules to cover the sidecar.

**What was implemented**

Failing tests first, in `test/unit/lint-boundary.js` (11 failures
against the old config) and `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`, then the
config.

- `eslint-boundary.config.js`: dropped the `src/renderer/webtorrent.js`
  override (US-045 deleted the file, so the exemption was pointing at
  nothing and would have silently exempted a file recreated at that
  path). `lib/cast.js` is the only exemption left.
- Split the ban list: `HOST` (`electron`, `@electron/remote`,
  `original-fs`) is now shared between the full renderer `RESTRICTED`
  set and two narrower ones.
- `lib/platform/ipc-engine.js` gets a per-file override that re-enables
  the Electron ban inside the otherwise-exempt shim glob. US-044 made
  it a WebSocket client, so it needs no host module; the Node half of
  the ban stays off because it is still a shim module.
- `src/engine/**/*.js` gets an override banning Electron and every DOM
  global (`no-restricted-globals`: window, document, navigator,
  localStorage, sessionStorage, XMLHttpRequest) while leaving Node
  builtins entirely alone. The engine's Electron message is its own --
  the renderer's "import it from lib/platform instead" is nonsense
  advice for a process with no shim -- and a test asserts that.
- `npm run lint:boundary` now lints `src/renderer src/engine`. A config
  override the script never runs over is decorative, so a test asserts
  the script still names both trees.
- `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`: the source-scan counterpart. Scans
  `src/engine/*.js` for Electron imports (both `import` and
  `require()`) and DOM globals, and scans `ipc-engine.js` explicitly,
  since every renderer walk in that file skips the shim directory.
- `test/unit/engine-ipc-call-sites.js` was **kept**, not deleted (AC-6
  allows either). It stubs `ipc-engine.js` itself, so no assertion in
  it ever named a transport, and it still describes exactly what it
  described before: which shim export each controller reaches with
  which arguments. Its header now says so and points at the two files
  that do cover the WebSocket transport.
- AGENTS.md updated in `src/engine/`, `src/renderer/lib/platform/` and
  `test/`.

**Files changed**

`eslint-boundary.config.js`, `package.json`,
`test/unit/lint-boundary.js`, `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`,
`test/unit/engine-ipc-call-sites.js`, `src/engine/AGENTS.md`,
`src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md`, `test/AGENTS.md`,
`specs/prd.json`.

Verified: `npm run lint` clean, `npm test` 1736/1736 pass. No app
launch was needed -- this story changes no shipped code.

**Learnings for future iterations:**

- **An ESLint `overrides` entry can tighten, not just exempt.** The
  shim's directory glob turns `no-restricted-imports` off wholesale; a
  later, more specific entry for one file inside it turns a narrower
  version back on. That is how a shim module that has *stopped*
  needing a host import gets held to it. Order matters -- the
  tightening entry has to come after the glob.
- **A per-file override list is not the same as an exemption list.**
  `lint-boundary.js` used to count per-file overrides and assert there
  were exactly two. With `ipc-engine.js` added that count is
  meaningless; the assertion now filters on
  `rules['no-restricted-imports'] === 'off'`. Any future "how many
  carve-outs are there" test should do the same.
- **`no-restricted-globals` works with no `env` set.** It matches
  references that resolve to the global scope *and* unresolved ones,
  which is exactly what a stray `window.x` in a Node file is. No
  `env: { browser: true }` is needed to make it fire, and adding one
  would be wrong here.
- **Verify a lint rule through the npm script too, not only the Node
  API.** `lintText()` proves the config; it cannot prove the script
  passes the directory to ESLint. Drop a throwaway violating file into
  the tree, run the real script, read the two errors, delete it. That
  is how the missing `src/engine` argument in `lint:boundary` surfaced.
- **A deleted file leaves a live exemption behind.** The
  `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` override survived US-045 because
  nothing fails when a glob matches no file -- and `lint-boundary.js`
  lints synthetic text at that path, so both checks stayed green.
  When a story deletes a file, grep the lint config and the boundary
  tests for its path.
