## 2026-07-29 - US-051

Scaffold the Tauri host: a second, launchable host alongside
`src/main/` (ADR-014), loading the same `static/main.html` and the same
Vite output Electron loads.

**What was implemented**

- `src-tauri/` as a Cargo crate: `Cargo.toml` (`tauri = "2"`,
  `tauri-build = "2"`, `serde_json` for tests, `rust-version =
  "1.77.2"`), `build.rs`, `src/main.rs`, `tauri.conf.json`, and
  `Cargo.lock` (committed -- it is a binary crate).
- One window, declared in `tauri.conf.json`: `static/main.html`,
  500x638 with the 425x238 minimum from `src/config.js`, background
  `#282828`. Bounds restore and the macOS title-bar inset are US-056's,
  not copied here.
- `bin/build-tauri.js` assembles `build-tauri/`, the document root the
  window loads, by copying `static/` and `build-vite/` into it side by
  side. It exports `{ COPY, OUT_DIR }` so the unit test asserts against
  the real list rather than a duplicated string.
- `npm run build:tauri` (renderer build + assembly) and
  `npm run start:tauri` (`build:tauri && tauri dev`). `npm start` is
  byte-identical to what it was, and a test holds it that way.
- `@tauri-apps/cli` 2.11.4 as an exact devDependency. depcheck finds it
  through the `tauri dev` in `start:tauri`, so it needs no ignore entry.
- The five registrations a new generated directory needs:
  `.gitignore` (plus `src-tauri/target/`), nodemon's `--ignore` list,
  depcheck's `--ignore-dirs`, the `rimraf` block in `bin/package.js`,
  and `bin/clean.js`. electron-packager's `ignore` regex already
  excludes `src-tauri/` -- `^\/src` matches it as a prefix.
- `test/unit/tauri-host.js` (19 assertions) and two `cargo test` cases
  in `main.rs`, both on the one property a launch depends on: the
  window URL has to sit at the same depth inside the document root that
  `static/main.html` sits at inside the repo.

**Files changed**

`src-tauri/{Cargo.toml,Cargo.lock,build.rs,tauri.conf.json,AGENTS.md}`,
`src-tauri/src/main.rs`, `bin/build-tauri.js`, `bin/clean.js`,
`bin/package.js`, `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `.gitignore`,
`test/unit/tauri-host.js`, `specs/prd.json`.

**Acceptance criterion 2 is half met, and the other half cannot be met
in this story**

Measured, not assumed. A probe build of `main.rs` (a `probe` command
plus an init script hooking `window.onerror`; recipe in
`src-tauri/AGENTS.md`) reported:

```
PROBE: ERROR global code@tauri://localhost/build-vite/renderer/main.js:1:1106
PROBE: DOM title=Main Window bodyLen=0 header=false scripts=1
```

So the window does open, and it does load `static/main.html` and the
existing Vite output -- the HTML rendered, and the webview fetched
`build-vite/renderer/main.js` through the asset protocol from the
assembled root. The UI does not render: offset 1106 is the bundle's
first `require("fs")`. The bundle is CJS with host externals (ADR-004)
and a Tauri webview has no `require`, so it throws at load, before any
host call.

Fixing that is not one flag away, and three later stories own the
pieces:

- **US-052** owns `WT_HOST` -- ESM/no-externals output is listed in
  ADR-014 under the build flag, and adding it here would front-run the
  story that has to select the shim tree with it.
- **US-054** owns the next failure after that one. With `require` gone
  the load reaches `application-config-path`, which calls
  `os.platform()` at import time; a browser-stubbed `os` throws there.
  `src/config.js`'s `CONFIG_PATH` is on the same path.
- **US-055** owns the one after. `State.load(onState)` is the first
  statement of `src/renderer/main.js` and `onState` does
  `if (err) return onError(err)`, so React never renders unless the
  config read succeeds -- and under US-052's rules every Tauri shim
  stub throws a not-implemented error rather than returning undefined.

That last point is worth stating plainly: US-051's "the UI renders,
even though host calls fail" and US-052's "every stub throws rather
than returning undefined" cannot both hold before US-055. The only way
to make this window render today is a stub that lies about succeeding,
which rule 6 forbids and US-052 forbids. Marked passing on the
scaffolding, with the render deferred to the stories that own it and
recorded in the story's `notes`.

**Learnings for future iterations**

- **Tauri embeds `frontendDist` at compile time, even in dev.**
  `tauri-codegen`'s `context_codegen` only skips embedding when
  `devUrl` is set; with a directory it calls `EmbeddedAssets::new` and
  panics if the path is missing. So `cargo build`, `cargo test` and
  `cargo clippy` in `src-tauri/` all fail in a fresh clone until
  `npm run build:tauri` has run, and a renderer change is not visible
  to `cargo run` until it is re-run. It also means the document root
  should stay small; ours is 6.5M, almost all of it `static/`.
- **The shared `static/main.html` is what forces the copied document
  root.** Its `<script src="../build-vite/renderer/main.js">` resolves
  on disk for Electron but has to resolve *inside* the asset root for
  Tauri, so the root mirrors the repo layout (`static/` +
  `build-vite/`) and the window URL is `static/main.html`, not
  `main.html`. Symlinks do not work -- the compile-time walk does not
  follow them. Do not "tidy" the root to a flat directory without
  changing that tag, and changing that tag means touching the Electron
  host.
- **An ESM bundle cannot be dropped into that HTML either.** The tag is
  a classic `<script src>`, so top-level `import`/`export` in the
  output would be a syntax error, and `type="module"` cannot be added
  while Electron loads the CJS build from the same file. ADR-014 says
  the Tauri bundle is ESM; whoever implements `WT_HOST` has to check
  the emitted output for top-level import/export statements and reach
  for `iife` if there are any. This is a real gap in the ADR, not a
  detail.
- **How to see anything at all in the Tauri webview:** there is no
  Electron-style `console-message` event and WKWebView says nothing on
  stdout. A throwaway `#[tauri::command]` plus
  `.append_invoke_initialization_script(...)` is the equivalent of the
  `build/main/index.js` patch trick, and an app-defined command needs
  no capability entry in v2. `screencapture` is not an option in a
  non-interactive session -- it fails with "could not create image from
  display".
- **`cargo clippy` after a `cargo build` recompiles the whole
  dependency tree** (~250 crates, minutes) because the clippy driver
  is a different rustc. Run it in the background and do not conclude it
  hung. The two also contend for `target/`'s lock, so do not run a
  `tauri dev` beside it.
- **The npm-side gates were indifferent to all of this.** `standard`
  and the boundary lint see no JS under `src-tauri/`, and depcheck
  picked `@tauri-apps/cli` up from the npm script by itself.
---

