  `**/platform/tauri**` under `src/renderer/`; the `require()` and
  backslash spellings are covered by a new scan in
  `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`.
- `test/unit/platform-tauri.js` -- tree parity, per-module export
  name/arity parity, every stub rejects, and the resolver's five
  behaviours. `test/unit/lint-boundary.js` gains the two new lint
  cases.
- Docs: `docs/platform-shim.md` (a two-implementation section and the
  lint section), `src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md`,
  `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`.

Verification: `npm test` 2100/2100 green; `npm run lint` clean; both
renderer builds succeed with zero `has been externalized` warnings;
the Electron launch check passes (`init: 798ms`, sidecar up, killed
afterwards, `pgrep` clean). The six-step manual smoke test was not run
-- this session is non-interactive.

**Interpretation notes (AC judgement calls, recorded per rule 2):**

- AC-1/AC-2 say "one stub module per existing shim module" and "every
  stub throws". `ipc-engine.js` is re-exported instead. It has had no
  host import since US-044 -- it is a WebSocket client of the sidecar,
  and `eslint-boundary.config.js` bans it from importing Electron --
  so there is nothing for a Tauri version to do differently, and **no
  Phase 4 story reimplements it**. Stubbing it would have taken the
  torrent engine out of the Tauri build for the whole phase with
  nothing scheduled to put it back, which ADR-014 calls a sequencing
  error rather than a plan. The parity test still covers it.
- ADR-014 also says the Tauri bundle should be **ESM with no host
  externals**. Deliberately not done here. `src/renderer/main.js` and
  `src/config.js` still reach `electron` / `@electron/remote`
  directly, so dropping the externals today fails the build outright.
  The ADR ties that change to the concerns migrating (US-054 for
  config paths, US-072 for the cutover), not to this story.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **A Vite `resolveId` plugin needs `enforce: 'pre'` or it never
    runs.** Vite inserts `vite:resolve` ahead of *normal* user
    plugins, so anything it can already answer -- which is every
    resolution -- never reaches a plugin without it. The plugin then
    calls `this.resolve(source, importer, { ...options, skipSelf:
    true })` to get the real id and rewrites the result.
  - **Requiring a built shim module in a unit test loads the real
    `@electron/remote`, which dies outside Electron** with `Error: No
    such binding was linked: electron_common_features`. Worse, the
    sibling test files that *do* stub it share one `require.cache`:
    caching `platform/window` here with this file's stub would hand it
    to `platform-window.js`, which loads later in the glob. When a
    test only needs export *shapes*, parse the ESM source instead of
    requiring it.
  - **An inline env var in an npm script is not portable.**
    `WT_HOST=tauri vite build` is POSIX shell only, and Windows is one
    of Phase 4's three targets. `bin/build-renderer.js` sets
    `process.env.WT_HOST` and then calls Vite's JS API
    (`import('vite').then(({ build }) => build())`) -- one process, one
    exit code, no shell.
  - **Match the resolved file, never the import specifier**, when
    swapping a directory of modules. The barrel imports its siblings
    relatively (`./clipboard`), so a `resolve.alias` entry misses them
    and emits a bundle with half of each tree in it -- and that
    failure *builds*, so nothing catches it until a call site throws.
  - **The two-tree parity test is what keeps Phase 4 honest.** Adding
    or renaming a shim export now means doing it in both trees or
    `test/unit/platform-tauri.js` fails. A new non-module file under
    `tauri/` has to go in that file's `HELPERS` list too.
