  `src/renderer/lib/platform/tauri/ipc-os.js`,
  `src/renderer/lib/platform/AGENTS.md`, `eslint-boundary.config.js`,
  `docs/platform-shim.md`, `package.json`,
  `test/unit/platform-tauri-ipc-os.js` (new), `test/unit/platform-tauri.js`,
  `test/unit/platform-boundary.js`, `test/unit/lint-boundary.js`,
  `test/unit/tauri-host.js`.
- Verified: `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D
  warnings`, `cargo test` (30 tests) and `npm test` (2177) all pass. The
  JS tests were confirmed red first by stashing the shim and config
  changes and rebuilding. Single instance was checked against the real
  binary: the first launch stays up, a second exits 0 immediately, and
  a `--hidden` launch runs without a crash. Whether the window is
  *visually* where it was left is for the US-073 verification pass.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **Tauri grants the renderer nothing without a capability file.**
    App-defined `#[tauri::command]`s are exempt, but any core API the
    renderer reaches through `@tauri-apps/api` -- `event.listen`
    included -- needs `src-tauri/capabilities/default.json`. The
    failure is a runtime permission error in a webview whose console
    does not reach stdout, so it looks like nothing happened.
  - **Tauri reports physical pixels; Electron's `getBounds()` is
    logical.** Anything that crosses the shim carrying a rectangle has
    to be divided by `scale_factor()` first, or a retina window
    doubles in size on every restart.
  - **A window declared in `tauri.conf.json` is created before
    `setup()` runs**, so runtime geometry means declaring it
    `"visible": false` and calling `show()` yourself -- the same shape
    as Electron's `show: false` plus `ready-to-show`. There is no need
    to build the window in Rust to control where it opens.
  - **The not-implemented sweep in `test/unit/platform-tauri.js` now
    has an `IMPLEMENTED` register.** Porting a Tauri shim export means
    adding it there (and giving it a test file), or the sweep fails on
    the export that no longer throws.
  - **`cargo` has network access here**, so a new crate is fine; the
    first `clippy`/`build` after one recompiles the tree (~2.5 min).
  - **The Tauri binary can be exercised non-interactively.**
    `npm run build:tauri && cargo build`, then run
    `src-tauri/target/debug/webtorrent-desktop` in the background: it
    is a single process (no Electron-style tree), `kill $PID` lands,
    and a second foreground launch returning immediately is exactly
    the single-instance assertion.
