### Two items of `src/main/menu.js` are deliberately absent

Both are macOS Edit-menu entries with no Tauri equivalent: **Delete**
(Electron's `delete` role) and the **Speech** submenu (`startspeaking`
/ `stopspeaking`). `muda`'s `PredefinedMenuItemType` has no variant for
either, and a normal item cannot stand in because there is nothing for
it to call. Cmd+Backspace still deletes in a text field; the menu entry
is what is missing. Windows and Linux Help > About opened a second
Electron window (`windows.about`), and there is no second window under
Tauri, so the About **role** stands in -- it shows the same name,
version and licence. All three are recorded in the module comment at
the top of `menu.rs`.

### Runtime evidence, taken with `osascript`

The "under `npm run start:tauri`" criterion did not have to wait for a
human. macOS accessibility scripting drives the real menu bar, so the
whole path was exercised from four background launches and the probe
was removed afterwards (`grep PROBE` over `main.rs` and `menu.rs` is
clean; the Rust gates were re-run after the revert).

- The menu bar renders as `getMenuTemplate` leaves it: WebTorrent,
  File, Edit, View, Playback, Transfers, Window, Help -- the macOS
  order, with Window between Transfers and Help. Accelerators are
  attached (Cmd+N, Cmd+O, Cmd+U, Ctrl+Cmd+F, Alt+Cmd+I and the rest).
- Accelerators fire the right action: Cmd+U -> `openTorrentAddress`
  (File), Cmd+, -> `preferences` (app menu), Esc -> `escapeBack`
  (View), Space -> `playPause` and N -> `nextTrack` (Playback).
  Transfers and Help have no accelerators in the Electron template
  either; Transfers was checked by clicking Pause All ->
  `pauseAllTorrents`. Edit and Window are roles, which the OS performs.
- `setAllowNav` and the player notifications were checked by their
  effect rather than by reading the menu: with `setAllowNav(false)` in
  force Cmd+, dispatched nothing, and after `setAllowNav(true)` the
  same keypress dispatched `preferences`. Space dispatched nothing
  before `notifyPlayerOpen()`, `playPause` after it, and nothing again
  after `notifyPlayerClose()`. After
  `notifyPlayerUpdate(hasNext: true, hasPrevious: false)`, N
  dispatched `nextTrack` and P dispatched nothing.
- The context menu round-trips: descriptors sent from the webview, a
  real popup on screen, and picking its first item came back as
  `contextMenuClick` with `{"menu":7,"item":0}` -- the serial number
  the shim sent, and the index it needs to find the callback it kept.
  `show_context_menu` returned immediately rather than blocking for the
  choice, which is the contract `Menu.popup({ window })` has under
  Electron.
- Not exercised: the Help items, which would open five URLs in the
  user's browser. `shell.openExternal` is already proven (US-059) and
  `the_help_items_open_the_configured_urls` pins the five URLs to
  `src/config.js`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **An accessibility read is not evidence; an accessibility
    *action* is.** Most of this session went into a bug that was not
    there: System Events reported the Playback items `enabled=false`
    after the host had enabled them, so `set_enabled` looked broken,
    and reading muda's and Tauri's source for the mechanism that could
    silently drop the update found nothing because nothing was
    dropping it. What settled it was pressing the accelerator: the
    disabled item dispatched nothing and the enabled one dispatched.
    The attribute was stale, and so was `name` -- a renamed item kept
    its old title. Now in the Patterns section.
  - **Check the previous session's working tree before starting.**
    This story's code was already written and uncommitted, with every
    gate passing. Reviewing and verifying it -- which is what turned
    up the missing `docs/platform-shim.md` rows -- was the work.
    Second time this has happened; it is in the patterns from US-060
    as "check the previous session's commit" and it applies to
    uncommitted changes just as much.
  - **A menu is the first host concern whose acceptance criterion the
    Rust tests genuinely cannot reach.** `template()` proves the
    descriptors and `action()` proves the mapping, but "is it in the
    menu bar and does the shortcut fire" is the OS's answer, and the
    two can disagree (an accelerator that fails to parse is dropped
    silently, which is exactly this shape of bug). Budget a real
    launch for it rather than treating the unit tests as the gate.
