  `refresh`, `destroy`) is at the bottom of the file.
- The tray menu is built from `menu::Item` descriptors through
  `menu::build_menu` (the old `build_context`, renamed -- a context
  menu and the tray's share "built once, never edited"). Ids are
  namespaced `tray:` so the three menus that arrive at the same event
  cannot collide; a test asserts `menu::action` and
  `menu::context_choice` both ignore them.
- Wired into `main.rs`: `tray::init` after the window is restored,
  `watch_close` for the close button, `show_main` / `hide_main` /
  `quit` as the three things a tray click can do, tray refresh from
  the focus watcher (Electron's `tray.setWindowFocus`), and
  `.build(...).run(|app, event| ...)` so `RunEvent::Exit` takes the
  icon down and `RunEvent::ExitRequested` sets the quitting flag.
- Files: `src-tauri/src/tray.rs` (new), `src-tauri/src/main.rs`,
  `src-tauri/src/menu.rs`, `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`,
  `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`, `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.
- Interpretation worth recording: the story says "port the tray", and
  the tray's only consumer elsewhere is `tray.hasTray()` in the
  window's close handler, so that handler came with it -- a tray that
  cannot be hidden to is not the ported behaviour. Its macOS branch
  did not: Electron hides on close there too and gets the window back
  from the dock, which is US-063, so macOS closes for now and the doc
  comment on `on_close` says why.
- Verified on the real binary, not only in `cargo test` (three
  launches, all cleaned up): the status item's menu read back
  "Hide to tray, Quit"; clicking Hide left 0 windows with the process
  alive and the menu relabelled "Show WebTorrent"; Show brought the
  window back; Quit exited and took the icon with it; the close button
  hid with a tray and exited without one.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **A `tauri` feature is how the tray and its icon exist at all.**
    `tray-icon` for `TrayIconBuilder`, and `image-png` / `image-ico`
    for `Image::from_bytes` -- without them the failure is a missing
    method on `AppHandle`, which reads like an API-version problem
    rather than a feature-flag one. Adding features recompiles the
    tree (~1.5 min).
  - **The icon has to be embedded.** `frontendDist` is walked into the
    binary at compile time, so `static/WebTorrent.png` is not a file
    at runtime in a packaged build; `include_bytes!` of the same asset
    is the port of `getIconPath`, and the test asserts the PNG and ICO
    magic rather than a path.
  - **Hiding instead of closing needs the quit flag from day one.**
    Electron carries `app.isQuitting` for exactly this, and without
    the Rust equivalent every route out of the app (menu Quit, tray
    Quit, the OS) is caught by the close handler and the app cannot be
    quit at all. `.run(|app, event| ...)` instead of `.run(context)`
    is what gives `ExitRequested` and `Exit`, and `Exit` is where the
    tray comes down.
  - **macOS AX reads of a *status* item are fresh**, unlike the menu
    bar's (US-061). Item names came back changed immediately after the
    window was hidden, so the tray can be asserted by name -- but the
    behavioural check (`count of windows`) is still the stronger one
    and cost nothing.
  - **A platform this machine is not can still be driven once.** macOS
    creates no tray, so the module was unobservable here until a
    throwaway env-var escape hatch in `init` forced it; that plus
    `osascript` on `menu bar 2` exercised the whole thing. Recipe in
    `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`. Grep the hatch out before committing, the
    same way US-060's probe command had to be.
