## 2026-08-04 - US-066
- Ported `src/main/power-save-blocker.js` to Rust. New
  `src-tauri/src/power.rs` holds `PowerSaveBlocker`, which is
  Electron's two `isStarted` guards -- enable does nothing if a block
  is already held, disable does nothing if none is -- over a `Wake`
  that each platform implements. `strategy(Platform)` is the pure
  decision, asserted for all three platforms: macOS takes an IOKit
  `PreventUserIdleDisplaySleep` assertion (the same one Electron's
  `prevent-display-sleep` takes), Windows holds `ES_DISPLAY_REQUIRED`
  on a thread of its own because that flag is per-thread and a command
  does not choose its thread, and Linux runs a `systemd-inhibit` child
  holding an `idle` lock.
- Release is `Drop` on the held inhibitor rather than a method, so
  every path -- `disable`, the window's `CloseRequested`, `RunEvent::
  Exit`, a panic -- gives the block back through the same code. The
  tests count starts against releases against a fake `Wake`, which is
  what "no leaked inhibitor" means here; removing either guard or
  leaking the token fails four of them (checked by mutation, not
  assumed).
- Wiring matches `src/main/ipc.js`: `notify_player_open` and the new
  `notify_player_play` enable, `notify_player_close` and the new
  `notify_player_pause` disable. `platform/tauri/ipc-os.js` implements
  `notifyPlayerPlay` and `notifyPlayerPause` against the two new
  commands and stops throwing `NotImplementedError`.
- One deliberate departure from Electron, commented at the call site:
  closing the window releases the block even when the close only hides
  the window to the tray. The renderer is not asked before the window
  goes, so it sends no `onPlayerClose`, and a hidden player has no
  visible video for the display to stay awake for.
- Files: `src-tauri/src/power.rs` (new), `src-tauri/src/main.rs`,
  `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`, `src-tauri/Cargo.lock`,
  `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`, `src/renderer/lib/platform/tauri/ipc-os.js`,
  `test/unit/platform-tauri-ipc-os.js`, `test/unit/platform-tauri.js`,
  `specs/prd.json`.
- Gates: `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D
  warnings`, `cargo test` (114 pass, 8 of them `power::`), and
  `npm test` (2325 pass). Plus three background launches with a
  throwaway `append_invoke_initialization_script` probe, removed
  before committing: `pmset -g assertions` showed exactly one
  `PreventUserIdleDisplaySleep named: "WebTorrent Desktop is playing
  media"` after open+play, none after pause, and none after a
  Cmd-W close taken while the block was held.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **`pmset -g assertions` is a non-stale read of a real host
    effect**, unlike the AX menu-item attributes. Recipe in
    `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`. Budget for the timing: the first webview
    load varies by seconds between launches, so a probe that releases
    the block 9s in raced two checks that disagreed. Hold it for 30s
    and check either side instead of tuning the sleep.
  - **Dead code is the tax on a "pure decision plus platform call"
    module that only one platform can run.** `strategy` and the
    per-platform constructors are all `pub` and all unused on two
    platforms out of three; `-D warnings` fails on it. Resolving it by
    `cfg`-ing the *constructors* and letting one `match` on `strategy`
    select between them keeps the decision in one place, and the
    unreachable arms return a `Wake` that refuses rather than
    `unreachable!()` -- a display that sleeps during playback is a
    smaller failure than a host that dies when the player opens.
  - **A test-only reader belongs behind `#[cfg(test)]`.**
    `is_enabled` is Electron's `isStarted` and nothing in the host
    asks it, because the guards are its only callers. Marking it
    rather than leaving it `pub` is what keeps it honest about that.
