## 2026-08-04 - US-067

- Ported `src/main/shortcuts.js` to the Tauri host. The new
  `src-tauri/src/shortcuts.rs` holds the three media keys as a table
  of accelerator/action pairs (`MEDIA_KEYS`), a `MediaShortcuts` guard
  that tracks which keys were actually taken, and a `Keys` trait whose
  real implementation is the `global-shortcut` plugin reached from
  Rust. Each press dispatches the renderer action Electron dispatches:
  `playPause`, `nextTrack`, `previousTrack`.
- Wired into `notify_player_open` and `notify_player_close`, beside
  the playback menu items and the power save blocker, so the keys are
  held only while a player is open. Both commands became `async` --
  see the learning below -- which forced a `Result<(), String>` return
  they always fill with `Ok(())`. `RunEvent::Exit` releases the keys
  too, and so does `Drop` on `MediaShortcuts`.
- The keys are taken through the plugin's **Rust** API, as with the
  dialog, opener and clipboard plugins, so
  `capabilities/default.json` stays at `core:default` for a sixth
  story and the webview carries no shortcut API of its own. No
  shortcut is declared on the plugin builder: `shortcuts.js` holds
  none until a player opens.
- Files: `src-tauri/src/shortcuts.rs` (new), `src-tauri/src/main.rs`,
  `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`, `src-tauri/Cargo.lock`,
  `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`, `progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`. Nothing on
  the JS side changed: `ipcOs.notifyPlayerOpen`/`notifyPlayerClose`
  already existed in both shim trees, and `src/main/shortcuts.js`
  stays until the cutover (US-072).
- Verified. `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D
  warnings` and `cargo test` (123 tests, 9 of them new) pass; `npm
  test` passes (2325 assertions). The nine new tests were
  mutation-checked rather than taken on trust: registering only the
  first key and draining nothing on release fails seven of them.
- Runtime evidence, taken with a throwaway probe (an injected script
  plus `probe_log`/`probe_keys` commands, both removed before
  committing): against the real host,
  `is_registered` for the three accelerators read `[false,false,false]`
  before the player, `[true,true,true]` after `notify_player_open`,
  and `[false,false,false]` after `notify_player_close`, with no
  registration failure on stderr. A second `notify_player_open` in
  between resolved rather than hanging, which is the deadlock check.
- What is left for a human (US-073): actually pressing a media key
  during playback and seeing the video respond, and confirming another
  application's play/pause works again once the player is closed. The
  mechanism underneath both is proven above.
- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **`append_invoke_initialization_script` has to go on the
    `Builder::default()` call.** Chained after the `.plugin(...)`
    calls instead, the script never ran and the probe printed
    nothing at all -- which reads exactly like a deadlock in the
    command being probed. Copy the working recipe out of US-060's
    `wip` commit (`git log -S PROBE_SCRIPT`) rather than writing a
    fresh one.
  - **A global shortcut is machine-wide state, so releasing it is the
    half that matters.** Registering is idempotent enough in practice;
    a host that exits holding play/pause leaves the machine broken in
    a way restarting *this* app does not fix. Hence `Drop`, the
    `RunEvent::Exit` release, and tracking the keys that were actually
    taken rather than a flag -- a key another application already owns
    is refused, and handing back one this process never had would
    report an error the user cannot act on.
  - **Check a plugin's added crates against ADR-014 before writing
    against its API.** `tauri-plugin-global-shortcut` 2.3.2 adds
    exactly three packages to the lockfile (`global-hotkey` 0.8,
    `xkeysym`), all at or below 1.77.2 -- cheap, but the way to know
    that is `cargo add` followed by a lockfile diff and a
    `cargo metadata` read of each new package's `rust_version`, not
    the crate's own `rust-version`.
