## 2026-08-04 - US-068 Port the folder watcher to Rust

- `src-tauri/src/watcher.rs` is `src/main/folder-watcher.js`: it reads
  the watcher's two preferences out of the renderer's `config.json`
  (`saved_prefs`, alongside `window::read_saved_state`), scans the
  configured folder recursively for `.torrent` files (`scan`), decides
  which of them are new and finished being written (`Watch`), and runs
  the polling thread the two commands start and stop
  (`FolderWatcher`).
- `main.rs` gains `start_folder_watcher` and `stop_folder_watcher`,
  both argument-free, as the Electron IPC handlers are. The watcher
  also comes up at launch when `autoAddTorrents` is already on, which
  is `delayedInit` in `src/main/index.js`, and is stopped on
  `RunEvent::Exit` beside the power blocker and the media keys. Each
  torrent found goes out as `dispatch('addTorrent', path)` -- the same
  action on the same channel the Electron host sends it on, so the
  renderer half is the `onDispatch` subscription it already had.
- `tauri/ipc-os.js` implements the two exports over `invoke`; they are
  registered in `test/unit/platform-tauri.js`'s `IMPLEMENTED` and
  covered in `test/unit/platform-tauri-ipc-os.js`.
- Files: `src-tauri/src/watcher.rs` (new), `src-tauri/src/main.rs`,
  `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`, `src/renderer/lib/platform/tauri/ipc-os.js`,
  `test/unit/platform-tauri-ipc-os.js`, `test/unit/platform-tauri.js`,
  `specs/prd.json`.
- Checked end to end in the real binary, one launch, with a throwaway
  `HOME` so the check had a profile of its own: the watcher started
  from the saved preference, reported a torrent dropped into a
  *subfolder* of the watched one, reported nothing after
  `stop_folder_watcher` arrived from the webview, and reported the
  next drop after `start_folder_watcher` arrived. The probe is
  reverted; `grep WATCH-PROBE` is clean.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **`chokidar` has no Rust equivalent worth taking here.** `notify`
    8 is the current line and its `notify-types` 2.1 needs rustc 1.85,
    over ADR-014's 1.77.2; `notify` 6 is inside it (everything at or
    below 1.75) but is a superseded major line and 20 packages. And
    neither gives `awaitWriteFinish`, which is the part that matters
    -- a half-written `.torrent` handed to the engine is a broken
    torrent -- so a size-stability poll has to be written either way.
    Once it is, what remains is a directory walk, which is `std`.
  - **`ignoreInitial` has to be sampled where the caller is, not on
    the new thread.** Taking the "what was already here" scan inside
    the spawned thread makes "is this file an addition or was it
    already there" a scheduling race, and the losing side is silent:
    the file is filed as pre-existing and never reported. The first
    version did that and its own test caught it.
  - **The saved state is re-read on every start.** The Electron main
    process loads `config.json` once at launch, so changing the
    watched folder there does not take effect until a restart; this
    host reads the file again in `watch_folder`, which is what makes
    "toggle the preference and it works" true without one.
  - A `pub` method used only by that module's tests fails
    `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` as dead code. That is usually the
    right answer rather than an `#[allow]`: the getter it wanted to
    keep (`watching()`) was a second answer to "which folder is being
    watched" with nothing keeping it honest, and the tests say it
    better by asserting which files the callback is handed.
