## 2026-08-04 - US-069 Port launch-on-login to Rust

- `src-tauri/src/startup.rs` is `src/main/startup.js`, which is the
  `auto-launch` package and nothing else. No platform has an API for
  this, so each one is a file or a registry value built as a pure
  function of an `Entry` (the executable, and the name to file it
  under): a `~/Library/LaunchAgents/<name>.plist` launch agent on
  macOS, a `~/.config/autostart/<name>.desktop` entry on Linux, and a
  `HKCU\...\CurrentVersion\Run` value written with `reg.exe` on
  Windows. All three compile on every platform. The two guards
  `startup.js` is made of -- install only when it is not installed,
  uninstall only when it is -- are `install`/`uninstall` over a
  `LoginItems` trait, which is also what makes them testable.
- `main.rs` gains `set_startup(startup: bool)`, `async` because the
  work is a file write or a child process. `tauri/ipc-os.js`'s
  `setStartup` invokes it, is registered in `IMPLEMENTED` in
  `test/unit/platform-tauri.js`, and is covered in
  `test/unit/platform-tauri-ipc-os.js`. Nothing else on the JS side
  changed: the preferences checkbox already calls this export.
- **The macOS branch deliberately differs from `startup.js`**, which
  gets `auto-launch`'s default -- a System Events login item. That
  verb is dead on this OS (macOS 26.5.1): `make login item at end with
  properties {...}` answers `login item <name>` and adds nothing, the
  item is absent from `every login item` two seconds later and from a
  fresh `osascript` after that, and deleting it fails with -1728. The
  same happens without the `name` and `hidden` properties, so it is
  the `LSSharedFileList` machinery behind the verb. The launch agent
  is the same package's other macOS branch and it works. The reason
  and the evidence are in the module's doc comment, because the next
  person to compare the two hosts will otherwise read it as a mistake.
- That choice also settles the `wasOpenedAsHidden` question
  `window.rs` had left open for this story: a launch agent passes
  `--hidden` as a real argument, so `is_hidden` reading argv is the
  whole test on all three platforms and Electron's macOS-only login
  item probe has nothing left to answer. The comment there says so.
- Files: `src-tauri/src/startup.rs` (new), `src-tauri/src/main.rs`,
  `src-tauri/src/window.rs`, `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`,
  `src/renderer/lib/platform/tauri/ipc-os.js`,
  `test/unit/platform-tauri.js`, `test/unit/platform-tauri-ipc-os.js`,
  `progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`.
- Verified. `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D
  warnings` and `cargo test` (147 tests, 13 of them new) pass, as does
  `npm test` (2329 assertions). The new tests were mutation-checked:
  dropping the install guard fails two, removing the whole directory
  instead of the file fails the round trip, and matching a login item
  name by substring failed the parser test the earlier draft had.
- Runtime evidence, two launches of the real binary with a throwaway
  `HOME` and a probe that invokes `set_startup` (both reverted; `grep
  PROBE` is clean). `set_startup(true)` wrote
  `$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents/webtorrent-desktop.plist` naming this
  binary and `--hidden`, and `plutil -lint` passes it.
  `set_startup(false)` removed exactly that file and left the
  directory. That second launch also carried `--hidden`, and System
  Events counted 0 windows for the process while it was up.
- What is left for a human (US-073): logging out and back in with the
  preference on, on each platform, and seeing the app come up without
  a window.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - The pattern above (an API Electron uses can be dead on the current
    OS) is the story's main one and is in `## Codebase Patterns`.
  - **A `--hidden` launch is checkable in one line.**
    `osascript -e 'tell application "System Events" to tell process
    "webtorrent-desktop" to count windows'` answers 0 while the host is
    up and hidden. Unlike the menu-item attribute reads, this is a
    count of real windows and was not stale.
  - **`append_invoke_initialization_script` takes an owned `String`,**
    so a probe that varies per launch can be `format!`ed or `.replace`d
    from an env var rather than needing two builds -- that is how one
    binary did both the install and the uninstall launch here.
  - **Two platforms' mechanisms turned out to be one.** `auto-launch`
    shares its `fileBasedUtilities` between macOS's launch agent and
    Linux's autostart entry, and following that gave the Linux branch
    real coverage on macOS: `FileEntries` is exercised against a temp
    directory for both file kinds, so the untestable platform here is
    only Windows.
