## 2026-08-04 - US-070 - Move sidecar supervision into the Rust host
- `src-tauri/src/engine.rs`: the Rust port of
  `src/main/engine-process.js`. Spawns `build/engine/index.js` under
  `node`, reads the one `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT=` stdout line, forwards
  the rest of the child's output to the host log, restarts on a crash
  with the same 500ms-doubling-to-8s policy over five attempts, resets
  that allowance when a port binds, gives up loudly, and answers
  `wait_for_endpoint` with the same `{ url, token }`. The token is
  `getrandom` in place of `crypto.randomBytes(24)`.
- `main.rs`: `mod engine`, the engine started in `setup()` (as
  `engineProcess.init()` is in `src/main/index.js`), the
  `get_engine_endpoint` command, and `engine.stop()` in
  `RunEvent::Exit`.
- `src/renderer/lib/platform/tauri/ipc-os.js`: `getEngineEndpoint` is
  no longer a stub -- one `invoke('get_engine_endpoint')`.
- `package.json`: `build:tauri` now runs `build:main`, because the
  sidecar it spawns is that script's output.
- Tests: six in `engine.rs` against a fake Node sidecar (endpoint,
  graceful stop, restart-after-crash, give-up, double stop) plus the
  pure parts; `getEngineEndpoint` in
  `test/unit/platform-tauri-ipc-os.js`; the build/handshake wiring in
  `test/unit/tauri-host.js`.
- Docs: `docs/platform-shim.md` and a new section in
  `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`.

**One acceptance criterion is not met, and cannot be met by this
story.** "Under `npm run start:tauri`, a torrent downloads, plays and
seeds" needs a renderer, and the Tauri renderer bundle does not boot:
an error probe in the real webview reports
`ReferenceError: Can't find variable: require` at load, because
`build-vite/renderer/main.js` still contains 27 `require("util")`,
`require("events")`, `require("fs")` ... calls that only Electron's
nodeIntegration answers. Nothing in this story's scope reaches that;
**US-072 (cut the app over to Tauri) owns it**, and it is recorded in
the PRD notes. What *was* verified end to end on the real host is
everything up to the renderer: the sidecar spawns and prints its port,
the webview's own `invoke('get_engine_endpoint')` comes back with
`{"url":"ws://127.0.0.1:53644","token":"7201..."}`, and no `node`
child survives either a `pkill` of the host or a real Cmd-Q.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **The parent pipe is a better kill than a signal, and it is
    already there.** `src/engine/index.js` shuts down when its stdin
    closes (`WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PARENT_PIPE=1`), which the Electron
    supervisor sets purely as an anti-orphan backstop. Using it as the
    *primary* stop means the Rust side needs no `libc`, no SIGTERM and
    no Windows branch: `drop(child.stdin.take())`, wait four seconds,
    then `Child::kill` as the backstop. Look for an existing
    cooperative shutdown before reaching for a signal crate.
  - **A supervisor is testable without the thing it supervises.** The
    whole contract with the child is one stdout line, an exit status
    and a stdin that closing means quit, so a few lines of Node in a
    temp directory exercise spawn, restart, give-up and graceful stop
    in half a second of `cargo test` -- no WebTorrent client, no port,
    no network. `node` was already a `cargo test` dependency
    (`paths.rs` shells out to it).
  - **Two mutexes, in a fixed order, keep a kill from blocking a
    read.** The child handle is held apart from the state a waiter
    reads, and never waited on while a lock is held (`try_wait` on a
    50ms poll instead). Locking state-then-child everywhere is what
    makes that safe, and the race worth guarding is the one between
    `spawn` and storing the child: the stopped flag is checked *while*
    storing, or a `stop()` landing in between leaves a process nobody
    holds.
  - **`CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR` is the dev build's answer to "where is the
    repo".** It is baked in at compile time, so it survives the binary
    being run from anywhere -- and is exactly wrong for a packaged
    build, which is why the production branch of `Runtime::resolve`
    takes the resource directory instead.
  - **Rust code only tests can call fails `-D warnings`.** Two
    getters mirroring the Electron module's `getEndpoint`/`getPid` are
    dead in the host, and dead code is a clippy error under the gate --
    `#[cfg(test)]` on the method, with a line saying why it exists, is
    the honest fix rather than an `allow`.
  - **The webview probe's `setTimeout` really is throttled.** The
    AGENTS.md warning is worth believing: a probe at 3000ms fired every
    time and the same probe moved to 8000ms produced total silence on a
    background launch.
