## 2026-08-04 - US-071 (port the updater to Tauri)

- `src-tauri/src/updater.rs`: the whole of `src/main/updater.js` as a
  pure decision (`feed_url`, `outcome`, `sysarch_for`, `platform_name`)
  plus one blocking `check` over `ureq`. `check_for_updates` in
  `main.rs` runs it on its own thread after the same
  `config.DELAYED_INIT` wait `delayedInit` makes, and dispatches
  `updateAvailable` on the channel the renderer already listens to.
  Nothing in `src/renderer/` changed: `controllers/update-controller.js`
  and the modal are untouched, and the shim needed no new export.
- `ureq = "=2.12.1"` added, pinned, with the MSRV reasoning in
  `Cargo.toml`.
- Tests: eleven in `updater.rs` -- the URL, the platform and width
  spellings, all four outcomes of a response, a stub feed on a
  loopback port answering an update and then a 204 through the real
  agent, and a port nothing listens on. `matches_src_config_js` shells
  out to node the way `paths.rs` does, so a change to
  `AUTO_UPDATE_URL`, `APP_VERSION`, `OS_SYSARCH` or `DELAYED_INIT` on
  the JS side alone fails `cargo test` -- including the `package.json`
  version bump the crate's version is supposed to follow by hand.
- Verified on the real host: two background launches against a local
  stub feed with a throwaway `HOME`, one answering 200 and one 204.
  The feed saw
  `/desktop/update?version=0.24.0&platform=darwin&sysarch=x64` both
  times; the webview received `["updateAvailable","9.9.9"]` on the
  dispatch channel in the first and nothing in the second. Probe and
  the feed-URL env hatch reverted afterwards.
- Files: `src-tauri/src/updater.rs` (new), `src-tauri/src/main.rs`,
  `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`, `src-tauri/Cargo.lock`,
  `src-tauri/AGENTS.md`, `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

**The acceptance criterion about the UI is met at the event, not at the
pixel.** `.modal` never appears, because the Tauri renderer bundle
still does not boot -- the same `ReferenceError: Can't find variable:
require` US-070 recorded, which **US-072 owns**. What was proved
instead is everything up to and including the webview: the host emits
the dispatch, and `updateAvailable` with the version is what arrives in
the page. The renderer half of that hop is the `onDispatch`
subscription the folder watcher already uses.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **`config.js`'s `arch()` is not `os.arch()`.** `OS_SYSARCH` reads
    the `arch` npm package, which answers whether the **OS** is 64-bit
    and only ever says `x64` or `x86` -- so an Apple-silicon machine
    asks the update feed for `x64`, and a port over `target_arch`
    would have asked for `ia32` on every arm build. Read the package,
    not the name, when mirroring a `config.js` value: the first cut of
    this was wrong and passed its test only because the Rust toolchain
    on this machine is `x86_64-apple-darwin` under Rosetta while node
    is native arm64. Two readings of "the architecture" on one machine
    disagree, so make the test compare the *decision* rather than a
    value both sides happen to derive the same way.
  - **A port can be more faithful by dropping a branch.**
    `src/main/updater.js` uses Electron's Squirrel `autoUpdater` on
    macOS and Windows, which silently downloads and installs; a
    like-for-like port would be a feature that does nothing at all
    until signing lands in Phase 5. Doing the Linux check on all three
    platforms delivers what the story asks for and is a smaller
    surface for US-085 to replace. The deviation is in the module's
    doc comment with what the user sees.
  - **`ureq` 2 is the HTTP shape this host wants.** Blocking, no async
    runtime, and it reports a 4xx/5xx as `Error::Status(code,
    response)` rather than a status on an `Ok` -- so the code that
    decides what a response means has to be reachable from both arms,
    which is why `outcome` takes a plain `(status, body)`.
  - **A stub HTTP feed is 30 lines of `std::net`.** `TcpListener` on
    `127.0.0.1:0`, read to the blank line, write a response with
    `Connection: close`, and hand the request line back to the test so
    it can assert on the query string. No dev-dependency, and it makes
    the network half of the module a unit test rather than something
    only a manual launch covers.
