## 2026-08-04 - US-072 - Cut the app over to Tauri

Finished the cutover that the two `wip` commits (`f46cabb`, `e8475db`)
left part-done, and verified it against a real launch rather than
against the tree.

What was already in place at HEAD and needed only checking: `src/main/`,
`index.js`, `bin/package.js`, `bin/shim-resolver.js`,
`bin/build-renderer.js` and the whole Spectron suite deleted;
`platform/tauri/` promoted to `platform/`; `WT_HOST` and the `resolveId`
plugin gone; every Electron package out of `package.json`; `build:main`
renamed `build:node`; the replacement plan for the integration suite
recorded in `test/AGENTS.md`; and `test/unit/electron-removed.js` as the
regression gate over all of it.

What was actually broken, in the order it was found:

- **`npm test` did not pass at HEAD.** `lib/migrations.js` had the new
  `copyIntoProfile` helper written and documented but not called: three
  call sites still joined `config.STATIC_PATH`, which no longer exists.
  Wired the helper in; the migrations tests that were written for it
  pass.
- **Nothing consumed `app_paths`.** `config.js` exported empty strings
  for `CONFIG_PATH` and its siblings and `src/renderer/main.js` called
  `State.load` straight away, so the profile was `./config.json`
  relative to nothing. Added `bootstrap()`: await
  `platform/paths.appPaths()`, `useHostPaths`, then `State.load`.
- **And the bootstrap called it through the wrong surface.** Only a
  launch caught this -- see the learnings below.
- Removed the `probe` command and the
  `append_invoke_initialization_script` block that `e8475db` left in
  `src-tauri/src/main.rs` (39 lines, deletions only).
- Documentation that the cutover had falsified: `src/AGENTS.md` still
  described externals, `nodeIntegration` and a `file://` document;
  ADR-004 (reversed), ADR-003 and ADR-010 (amended) had no note saying
  the ground had moved.

Verified by launch, `HOME=/tmp/wt-072`: the window renders (header,
five default torrents, 11KB of DOM), the host's `app_paths` answer is
what the profile is built at, and `config.json`, `Torrents/` and
`Posters/` are written there with the five bundled torrents and their
posters. One error remains at the 6s `delayedInit`, from
`Cast.init()` -> `util.inherits` in the casting stack: that is the
PRD's deferred casualty (ADR-008), it fires after render, and US-076
through US-079 own it.

Files changed: `src/renderer/main.js`, `src/renderer/lib/migrations.js`,
`src-tauri/src/main.rs`, `test/unit/renderer-boots.js`, `src/AGENTS.md`,
`bin/build-tauri.js`, `docs/adr/ADR-003`, `ADR-004`, `ADR-010`,
`specs/prd.json`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **A green sandbox is not a launch, and the gap is exactly one
    tick.** `test/unit/renderer-boots.js` answers `invoke` with a
    promise that never settles, so it proves the *synchronous*
    initialization of the bundle and nothing after the first host
    call. The bootstrap's `useHostPaths` was reached through the
    default export -- which carries the path values and deliberately
    not the function that moves them -- and that threw on a promise
    nobody awaited: no render, no message, and every unit test green.
    The fix is a second boot test that **answers one command** and
    leaves the rest pending, so startup advances exactly one step and
    the answer's use is assertable. Capture `unhandledRejection`
    around it: a bootstrap with no `catch` (which is right -- there is
    no UI to report into that early) otherwise takes the `tape`
    process down from a later tick.
  - **`npm run build:tauri` alone does not change what the binary
    runs.** `tauri.conf.json` points `frontendDist` at `build-tauri/`
    and `generate_context!` embeds it at *compile* time, so a renderer
    fix needs `cargo build` after it. Two launches in a row reported a
    bug I had already fixed before this was obvious -- if a launch
    contradicts a passing unit test, check the binary's age first.
  - **A throwaway probe left in `main.rs` is worth grepping for at the
    *start* of a session, not just before committing.** The one
    `e8475db` left behind is what made this session's diagnosis cheap,
    and it would equally have shipped. `grep -n "probe\|append_invoke"
    src-tauri/src/main.rs` is the check, and it belongs in both places.
  - **A `wip` commit is not a finished story.** HEAD failed `npm run
    lint`, and the failure (`copyIntoProfile is defined but never
    used`) was pointing straight at the unfinished work. Run `npm test`
    before reading anything else when a session inherits a dirty
    handover: the first failure is usually the map.
  - **Deleting a mechanism falsifies the documents that explain it.**
    ADR-004 decided CommonJS output with host externals; the cutover
    reverses both halves, and an ADR that still reads as a rule is
    worse than no ADR. The convention this repo has settled on is a
    dated banner under the title -- **Reversed by**, **Amended by** or
    **Spent as of** -- that says which story moved it and what is still
    live in the record, leaving the body intact as history.
---

## 2026-08-04 - US-074 - Record the casting architecture decision

Wrote `docs/adr/ADR-015-casting-without-node.md`, listed it in
`docs/adr/INDEX.md`, and put a dated **Amended by US-074** banner on
ADR-008 pointing at it. Documentation only, so no test was written:
the acceptance criteria are all "this document says X", and a test
asserting on the text of a document is one this repo's standing
instructions forbid. No code changed.

What the ADR decides, in short: casting moves to the Rust host behind
`lib/platform/cast.js`; Chromecast over the `rust_cast` crate, DLNA
and AirPlay reimplemented; no protocol dropped; subtitles served by
the host from a one-route `std::net` listener; Chromecast first, then
DLNA, then AirPlay; an unimplemented protocol reports no devices and
the existing button rule hides it.

Files changed: `docs/adr/ADR-015-casting-without-node.md` (new),
`docs/adr/INDEX.md`, `docs/adr/ADR-008-deferred-casualties-exempt-from-the-shim.md`,
`progress.log`, `specs/prd.json`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - **The cheap answer to "where does casting run" is the sidecar,
    and it is wrong for a recorded reason.** The engine sidecar is a
    real Node process, so `chromecasts`/`dlnacasts`/`airplayer` would
    keep working there with no protocol code written at all. Two
    earlier decisions rule it out: ADR-012 schedules the sidecar for
    replacement by librqbit, so device control put there is written
    onto code being deleted (ADR-008's own argument), and ADR-006
    keeps OS IPC off the `wt-*` engine channel. Expect this
    temptation to recur for anything else that wants Node.
  - **Check a candidate crate's build tree, not just its runtime
    tree.** The `cargo new /tmp/x && cargo add <crate> && cargo
    metadata` procedure prints both, and `rust_cast` 0.21 is the case
    that separates them: its runtime tree tops out at rustc 1.85
    (under the 1.86 Tauri already forces), but its build script runs
    `protobuf-codegen`, which pulls `which` -> `home` at MSRV 1.88.
    `cargo tree -i <crate>` says which side a package is on -- the
    `[build-dependencies]` marker appears in the inverted tree.
  - **Two rustls crypto providers in one process is a real hazard
    here.** The tree already compiles `rustls` 0.23.43 with `ring`
    underneath `ureq` (the updater). `rust_cast` resolves the same
    `rustls` with `aws-lc-rs`, whose `aws-lc-sys` is a C build.
    Linking both leaves rustls without an unambiguous process default,
    so US-076 has to install one explicitly and re-prove the *update
    check*, not just the cast path -- the failure would appear
    nowhere near the code that caused it.
  - **`cargo search` is available and settles "does a crate for this
    exist".** It is how the AirPlay decision got made honestly: every
    AirPlay crate on crates.io (`shairplay`, `rairplay`,
    `openairplay1/2`) is a *receiver*, so there is no sender to adopt
    and the reimplementation is forced rather than preferred.
  - **A UI can be kept unchanged across an async port by mirroring
    state.** `player-page.js` reads `state.devices[type].getDevices()`
    synchronously and `playback-controller.js` uses `Cast.setRate()`
    for its boolean return. The shim is all-async (ADR-005), so the
    bridge is a mirror in `main.js` rebuilt from host events, with the
    synchronous answers coming from a `capabilities` array on each
    mirrored device. Same trick should work for any other sync UI read
    the shim would otherwise break.
